---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// FLIP SWITCH: set to true when v0.2.0 ships with macOS assets
// (Apple Silicon .dmg attached to the GitHub release). Everything else —
// the .dmg download row with the Gatekeeper note — is already wired below
// and replaces the "build from source" row automatically.
const MACOS_RELEASED = true;
---

<Base
  title="Notare — local-first meeting notes and dictation"
  description="Your meetings, transcribed and enhanced — locally. Saved as plain Markdown you own. Open source, zero telemetry, no accounts."
>
  <!-- ============ hero ============ -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="orb" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <h1>
        Your meetings, transcribed and enhanced — <span class="accent">locally</span>.
      </h1>
      <p class="sub">
        Saved as plain Markdown you own. Notare records your meetings without
        bots, transcribes them on your GPU, and sharpens <em>your</em> notes
        with the transcript as context. Open source. No cloud. No account.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#download">
          Download for Windows · macOS · Linux
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare">View source on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <p class="meta-line">
        <span><span class="dot-ok" aria-hidden="true"></span> MIT licensed</span>
        <span><span class="dot-ok" aria-hidden="true"></span> Zero telemetry — compiled out</span>
        <span><span class="dot-ok" aria-hidden="true"></span> No accounts, ever</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ features ============ -->
  <section class="band" id="features">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">How it works</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">A precision instrument, not another AI subscription.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        No meeting bots joining your calls. No audio leaving your machine.
        Notare listens where you are, and everything it produces is a file on
        your disk.
      </p>

      <div class="feature-list">
        <div class="feature-row">
          <div>
            <h3><span class="idx">01</span> Local transcription, on your GPU</h3>
            <p>
              Whisper runs on-device with GPU acceleration — Metal, CUDA, or
              Vulkan. Every model is checksum-verified before it ever loads.
              Your audio never crosses the network; there is no server to
              trust because there is no server.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="vignette" role="img" aria-label="Live transcript mock: recording indicator, timer, and streaming speaker lines">
            <div class="vhead">
              <span class="rec-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="timer">00:24:<b>17</b></span>
              <span>Weekly product sync</span>
            </div>
            <div class="tline">
              <span class="tstamp">24:02</span>
              <span class="said">…so the migration lands behind the flag first, then we watch the error budget for a week.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="tline live">
              <span class="tstamp">24:15</span>
              <span class="said">Agreed — and the rollback path is just flipping the flag back</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="feature-row">
          <div>
            <h3><span class="idx">02</span> Notepad-first enhancement</h3>
            <p>
              You take rough notes during the meeting, like you always have.
              Afterwards, Notare enhances <em>your</em> notes — filling gaps,
              fixing names, attaching decisions — using the transcript as
              context. It sharpens what you wrote; it doesn't replace you with
              a summary bot.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="vignette" role="img" aria-label="Notes mock: a rough note enhanced with detail from the transcript">
            <div class="vhead"><span>notes · enhanced</span></div>
            <div class="mdblock"><span class="h">## Decisions</span>
- migration behind flag<div class="enh">- Migration ships behind <code>orders_v2</code> flag;
  1-week error-budget watch. Rollback =
  flag off. Owner: Priya, by Fri.</div></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="feature-row">
          <div>
            <h3><span class="idx">03</span> Plain Markdown, straight into your vault</h3>
            <p>
              Every note and transcript is a plain <code>.md</code> file saved
              to any folder you choose — including your Obsidian vault. No
              proprietary database, no export button, no lock-in. If Notare
              disappeared tomorrow, your notes wouldn't notice.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="vignette" role="img" aria-label="Markdown file mock showing frontmatter and wikilinks">
            <div class="vhead"><span>~/vault/Meetings/2026-07-16-Product-Sync.md</span></div>
            <div class="mdblock">---
date: 2026-07-16
attendees: [Priya, Sam, me]
---
<span class="h"># Product Sync</span>
Linked: <span class="link">[[Orders-V2-Migration]]</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="feature-row">
          <div>
            <h3><span class="idx">04</span> Bring your own everything</h3>
            <p>
              Enhancement runs on the LLM you point it at — Ollama on your own
              hardware, or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint with your own key.
              Calendar sync uses <em>your</em> Google OAuth credentials,
              straight from your machine to Google. No middleman, no metering,
              no one in between.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="vignette" role="img" aria-label="Settings mock: local Whisper model verified, Ollama endpoint connected, calendar via own OAuth">
            <dl class="kv">
              <dt>STT</dt><dd>whisper-small-q8 · local <span class="ok">✓ verified</span></dd>
              <dt>LLM</dt><dd>ollama · http://localhost:11434 <span class="ok">✓</span></dd>
              <dt>Calendar</dt><dd>Google · your OAuth client <span class="ok">✓</span></dd>
              <dt>Telemetry</dt><dd>not compiled in</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ covenant ============ -->
  <section class="band" id="covenant">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The covenant</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Free means free. In writing.</h2>
      <div class="covenant">
        <blockquote>
          Everything that works locally is <strong>free. Forever.</strong>
          No feature you have today will ever move behind a paywall.
          Notare+ will sell convenience — hosted sync and models —
          <strong>never capability.</strong>
        </blockquote>
        <p class="signed">
          — The Notare covenant. It's in the repo, under version control,
          like everything else. <a href="/plus">See exactly what Notare+ will charge for →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ download ============ -->
  <section class="band" id="download">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Download</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Get Notare</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Every link below always serves the newest release — no version numbers
        to chase. Grab it once; updates land in the same place.
      </p>

      <div class="ledger">
        <div class="ledger-row">
          <div>
            <h3>Windows</h3>
            <span class="tag">.msi installer</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Standard MSI installer for Windows 10/11.
            <br /><br />
            Note: the installer isn't code-signed yet, so SmartScreen will show
            an "unrecognized app" warning — click <em>More info → Run anyway</em>.
            The build is reproducible from the MIT source if you'd rather verify
            than trust.
            <span class="dl-actions">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare/releases/latest">Download .msi — latest release</a>
            </span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ledger-row">
          <div>
            <h3>Linux</h3>
            <span class="tag">.AppImage / .deb</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Portable AppImage (any distro — <code>chmod +x</code> and run) or a
            .deb package for Debian and Ubuntu. Local Whisper transcription with
            Vulkan and CUDA acceleration works out of the box.
            <span class="dl-actions">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare/releases/latest">Download .AppImage / .deb — latest release</a>
            </span>
          </p>
        </div>
        {
          MACOS_RELEASED ? (
            <div class="ledger-row">
              <div>
                <h3>macOS</h3>
                <span class="tag">.dmg · Apple Silicon</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Disk image for Apple Silicon Macs (M1 and later), with
                Metal-accelerated Whisper out of the box.
                <br />
                <br />
                Note: the app isn't notarized yet, so Gatekeeper will refuse a
                normal double-click the first time — <em>right-click the app →
                Open → Open</em> once, and it launches normally after that. The
                build is reproducible from the MIT source if you'd rather
                verify than trust.
                <span class="dl-actions">
                  <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare/releases/latest">
                    Download .dmg — latest release
                  </a>
                </span>
              </p>
            </div>
          ) : (
            <div class="ledger-row">
              <div>
                <h3>macOS</h3>
                <span class="tag">build from source</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Prebuilt macOS binaries aren't published yet — they're coming.
                Until then, Notare builds cleanly from source on Apple Silicon
                and Intel Macs (Metal-accelerated Whisper included).
                <span class="dl-actions">
                  <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare/blob/main/docs/BUILDING.md">
                    Build instructions — docs/BUILDING.md
                  </a>
                </span>
              </p>
            </div>
          )
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ closing cta ============ -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap waitlist" style="padding-top: 24px; padding-bottom: 24px;">
      <h2 class="section-title" style="max-width: none;">Own your meeting notes again.</h2>
      <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top: 30px; display: flex; gap: 14px; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap;">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare/releases/latest">Download Notare</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare">Star on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
